data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 10, the Cosmos DB provider deprecated synchronous LINQ operations but allowed opting back in via sync-over-async. **In EF Core 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.** Calling any synchronous LINQ operator (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`) or `SaveChanges()` on the Cosmos provider now throws an `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally.

**Every method in your repository breaks.** You must convert to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return types become `Task<T>` / `Task`
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`, `First()` → `FirstAsync()`, `Count()` → `CountAsync()`, `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`
- `Add()` stays synchronous (it's an in-memory tracking call, not I/O)
- Callers must propagate `async`/`await` up the call chain

This is **Cosmos-specific** — the relational providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, etc.) still support sync APIs.
